        "content": "```json\n[\n  {\n    \"groupIndex\": 1,\n    \"groupName\": \"prisma_migrate_development\",\n    \"summary\": \"This group consists of two Prisma Migrate tools designed for managing database schema migrations during development. The `prisma-migrate-dev` tool is used to synchronize the database schema with the Prisma schema file (`schema.prisma`). It performs a multi-step process: first, it re-runs existing migrations in a shadow database to detect any drift caused by manual edits or deleted migration files; second, it applies any pending migrations from colleagues; third, it generates a new migration from recent schema changes; fourth, it applies all unapplied migrations to the development database; and finally, it triggers artifact generation (e.g., Prisma Client). The complementary tool, `prisma-migrate-reset`, is used to forcefully reset the database and migration history when drift is detected or a clean slate is needed. It drops the database (or performs a soft reset if dropping is not possible), creates a new schema, applies all migrations, and runs seed scripts. This reset tool should only be used on development databases and never on production. Together, these tools enable a smooth development workflow where schema changes can be iterated and validated quickly, with the ability to start fresh when migration history becomes inconsistent.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"groupIndex\": 2,\n    \"groupName\": \"prisma_platform_setup\",\n    \"summary\": \"This group contains tools for setting up and managing online Prisma Postgres databases via the Prisma Platform. The `prisma-platform-login` tool handles authentication by logging in an existing user or creating a new account, which is a prerequisite for using Prisma Postgres services. The `prisma-postgres-create-database` tool allows the creation of a new online Postgres database, requiring a user-specified name and a region selection from a list that includes us-east-1, eu-west-3, ap-northeast-1, ap-southeast-1, or by calling without a region to get updated options. If the workspace plan limit is reached, the tool returns guidance: the user can either connect to an existing database via the Prisma Console (console.prisma.io) to obtain a connection string, upgrade their plan, or delete unused databases. These tools work sequentially: first ensure the user is logged in, then create the database in an appropriate region. They are essential for initializing a Prisma Postgres environment for development or production use.\"\n  }\n]\n```"
